improve testing

Isolation

Given two different buyers, when A verifies, then B’s entitlements are not in the response. This is the most important security test, so write it early.
Given an email that differs only by case or surrounding whitespace, when verify succeeds, then it still matches the same buyer.

Verify edge cases

Given an email with no purchases, when verify runs, then the response is identical to the success response (no enumeration) and no session carries entitlements.
Given an expired or already-used verification token, when verify runs, then no session is created.
Given a pending order (webhook not landed yet), when verify runs, then the response doesn’t claim failure for the other orders.

Session

Given an invalid or expired cookie, when the session is fetched, then authenticated=false.
Given the cookie value, then only the hash is stored (assert the raw token isn’t in the database).

tests

Traffic-light tests to add
Email grant returns all. Given two orders with the same email, when the session has an email grant, then both entitlements are returned.
Order grant doesn’t leak. Given two orders with the same email, when the session has only an order grant for one, then only that entitlement is returned. This is your $4 attack test, so it should be the first one written.
No duplicates. Given a session with both grants, then each entitlement appears once.
Idempotent grants. Granting the same email twice, or the same order twice, creates one row each.
Normalization. An email grant stored as Foo@Bar.com matches an entitlement stored as foo@bar.com.
Isolation. An email grant for A returns nothing from B.
Expiry. An expired session returns nothing for either grant type, and granting slides the expiry for both.
Late purchase. A new order with the same email after the grant appears in the next fetch for email sessions but not for order sessions.

Issues I saw in the handlers

The panic!("") in verify_magic_link. If the entitlement is missing, the Worker dies with an opaque 500 after the link has already been consumed. Return a proper error instead.
Mail outages leak who has bought. send_magic_link(...).await? fails with a 500 for known emails when Resend is down, while unknown emails still get 200. It also ignores Resend’s HTTP status, so a 4xx or 5xx loses the email silently. I’d check the status, log failures, and still return the generic 200.
Email scanners can burn the token. Outlook Safelinks and antivirus tools often GET links in emails before the user clicks, and your GET consumes the token, so the user sees “invalid or expired”. The usual fix is for /verify to be a page with a button that POSTs the token.
The 15 minutes in the email is hardcoded while the TTL comes from MAGIC_LINK_TTL_MINUTES, so they can drift apart.
The link points at {APP_ORIGIN}/verify, but the API route is /api/verify. I assumed something forwards one to the other. If nothing does, the link in the email is dead, and the SvelteKit /verify page doesn’t exist yet.

Number formatting: "1000 days" sits next to "1,000 weeks", so make them both "1,000".
Date input: the field has no label, so people won't know whether to enter a birthday or a first date. A label like "When did it start?" solves the "what is it" problem at the point of use.
Card label: "Ascending sequence" sounds like jargon. Something like "Counting up: 1-2-3-4-5-6" is friendlier and more fun.
Pricing: "Prints are always available either way" is unclear, because I can't tell what a print is. Also say whether $4 is one-time and per date, since "for that date" suggests it is.
Tagline: "The anniversaries you didn't know you had" would fit as a subhead or under the logo.
Chips: "Bitcoin launched" is ambiguous, since the genesis block and the whitepaper have different dates. Consider adding a personal chip like "My birthday" to show the main use.

3. Since you're forming the company

Add "by Emessy Labs" to the footer.
You're selling at $4 from France, so you'll probably need legal notices (mentions légales) and terms of sale (CGV) linked in the footer, and a payment setup that handles EU VAT. Merchants of record such as Paddle or Polar take care of that. Please verify these with an accountant, as I'm not one.

